  "provenance": [
    {
      "source_type": "manufacturer-pdf",
      "source": "Topps official checklist PDF: 2021series1baseball.pdf",
      "retrieved": "2026-08-07",
      "covers": "Series 1 (#1-330) and its inserts",
      "notes": "Topps prints \"Chicage White Sox\" on 86BC-9 and 86BC-16 and \"Montreal Expos\" unaccented on 70YTC-36, and both forms are in teams.json because rows record what is printed. Combo cards are tailed \"Combo\" here where other years print \"Combo Card\"."
    },
    {
      "source_type": "manufacturer-pdf",
      "source": "Topps official checklist PDF: 2021toppsseries2checklist.pdf",
      "retrieved": "2026-08-07",
      "covers": "Series 2 (#331-660) and its inserts",
      "notes": "Five sections Topps heads CHROME VARIATION are inserts here, because each numbers itself rather than reusing what it varies: 1986 Topps Baseball Chrome runs 86BC-1 to 86BC-200, Topps 1952 Redux Chrome 51BT-1 to 51BT-75, Topps 1965 Redux Chrome TC65-1 to TC65-50, and the two 70 Years of Topps Baseball Chrome lists 70YTC-1 to 70YTC-70 against the insert's 70YT-1 to 70YT-70. A variation shares the numbering of what it varies; these introduce 465 card numbers between them. Which series each belongs to was settled by its players rather than its numbers, since the two 70 Years Chrome lists use identical numbers and disagree on all seventy: the first names the Series 1 insert's players on 69 of 70 and the Series 2 insert's on none, and the second the exact reverse. The same test placed the 70 Years autographs, which match the Series 1 insert on 50 of 52 rows and Series 2 on none, so they vary Series 1. Four other autograph lists overlap their parent without being contained by it — Spring Training Cap Logo shares 33 of its 39 numbers, Topps 70th Anniversary Logo Patch 17 of 32 — so they introduce cards and declare no varies."
    }
  ]
